<div class="sasha-practice-page">

  <div class="sasha-container">


    <!-- ============================================
         HEADER
    ============================================= -->

    <header class="sasha-practice-page-hero">

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-eyebrow">
        Sasha Best Practice
      </p>

      <h1>
        Proposal Proofreading Process
      </h1>

      <p class="sasha-practice-page-intro">
        Give the final proposal a disciplined,
        detail-focused review before submission.
        Proofreading is the last opportunity to catch
        small errors, inconsistencies, formatting problems,
        and production issues that can undermine an
        otherwise strong proposal.
      </p>

    </header>


    <!-- ============================================
         PURPOSE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Proofreading deserves its own process
        </h2>

        <p>
          Proofreading is different from a Red Team Review.
        </p>

        <p>
          Red Team asks whether the substantially complete
          proposal is competitive. Proofreading focuses on
          the small details that remain after the major
          strategic and content decisions have been made.
        </p>

        <p>
          These details matter. A proposal containing
          inconsistent names, incorrect dates, spelling
          errors, broken tables, missing page numbers, or
          careless formatting can make an otherwise capable
          team appear less disciplined.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            The proofreading question
          </strong>

          <span>
            Is every detail of this proposal accurate,
            consistent, readable, and ready to submit?
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         DETAIL GODS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Find your detail gods
        </h2>

        <p>
          Some people naturally notice things other people
          miss.
        </p>

        <p>
          They see the table edge that is slightly out of
          alignment. They notice a name spelled differently
          on page 42. They spot the one American spelling
          in a document otherwise written in Canadian
          English.
        </p>

        <p>
          These people are extremely valuable during final
          proposal production.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

          <strong>
            Recruit them
          </strong>

          <span>
            Build a small group of reliable proofreaders
            who genuinely enjoy finding tiny mistakes.
          </span>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         REVIEWERS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Divide the proofreading work
        </h2>

        <p>
          One person trying to check everything at once
          will miss things.
        </p>

        <p>
          Where time permits, divide the final review into
          specific passes or responsibilities.
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-role-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Language proofreader
            </strong>

            <span>
              Reviews spelling, grammar, punctuation,
              capitalization, wording, and language
              consistency.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Consistency proofreader
            </strong>

            <span>
              Checks names, titles, terminology, dates,
              numbers, acronyms, project information,
              and repeated facts.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Visual proofreader
            </strong>

            <span>
              Reviews tables, headings, graphics,
              captions, alignment, spacing, pagination,
              and page appearance.
            </span>

          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-role">

            <strong>
              Sasha
            </strong>

            <span>
              Helps identify language errors,
              inconsistencies, repeated information,
              questionable facts, and items that deserve
              human verification.
            </span>

          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         PROCESS
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-page-heading-row">

        <h2>
          Conduct the proofreading process
        </h2>

      </div>


      <div class="sasha-practice-step-list">


        <!-- STEP 1 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            01
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Start with the final assembled document
            </h3>

            <p>
              Proofread the version that is actually being
              prepared for submission.
            </p>

            <p>
              Do not rely only on individual Word files,
              draft sections, or earlier versions.
            </p>

            <p>
              Assembly can introduce new problems:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                broken page references;
              </li>

              <li>
                missing text;
              </li>

              <li>
                altered formatting;
              </li>

              <li>
                incorrect pagination;
              </li>

              <li>
                misplaced graphics; and
              </li>

              <li>
                inconsistent headers or footers.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 2 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            02
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check spelling and language
            </h3>

            <p>
              Review the proposal carefully for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                spelling;
              </li>

              <li>
                grammar;
              </li>

              <li>
                punctuation;
              </li>

              <li>
                capitalization;
              </li>

              <li>
                missing or repeated words;
              </li>

              <li>
                awkward sentence construction;
              </li>

              <li>
                accidental fragments;
              </li>

              <li>
                inconsistent tense; and
              </li>

              <li>
                regional spelling conventions.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Watch regional spelling
              </strong>

              <span>
                If the organization uses Canadian English,
                make sure American spellings have not
                slipped into individual sections.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 3 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            03
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check names and titles
            </h3>

            <p>
              Names are especially easy to overlook because
              familiar words tend to be read rather than
              examined.
            </p>

            <p>
              Verify:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                client name;
              </li>

              <li>
                project name;
              </li>

              <li>
                RFP or procurement number;
              </li>

              <li>
                employee names;
              </li>

              <li>
                professional designations;
              </li>

              <li>
                job titles;
              </li>

              <li>
                subconsultant names; and
              </li>

              <li>
                agency and stakeholder names.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
              Check every occurrence, not just the first.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 4 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            04
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Verify dates, numbers, and quantities
            </h3>

            <p>
              Numbers can be grammatically perfect and
              still be wrong.
            </p>

            <p>
              Review:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                submission date and time;
              </li>

              <li>
                project dates;
              </li>

              <li>
                years of experience;
              </li>

              <li>
                project values;
              </li>

              <li>
                quantities;
              </li>

              <li>
                percentages;
              </li>

              <li>
                schedule durations;
              </li>

              <li>
                fee references; and
              </li>

              <li>
                page references.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                Never assume a number is right
              </strong>

              <span>
                If a figure matters, confirm it against
                the source information.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 5 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            05
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check terminology and acronyms
            </h3>

            <p>
              Make sure the proposal uses terminology
              consistently throughout.
            </p>

            <p>
              Check that:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                the same project phase is not called
                different things in different sections;
              </li>

              <li>
                roles use consistent titles;
              </li>

              <li>
                technical terms are used consistently;
              </li>

              <li>
                acronyms are defined where required;
              </li>

              <li>
                acronyms do not change form; and
              </li>

              <li>
                client terminology is preserved where
                appropriate.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 6 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            06
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check repeated facts
            </h3>

            <p>
              The same fact may appear in a resume,
              methodology, organization chart, project
              example, executive content, table, and
              graphic.
            </p>

            <p>
              Compare repeated information carefully.
            </p>

            <p>
              Look especially for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                different years of experience;
              </li>

              <li>
                conflicting project values;
              </li>

              <li>
                inconsistent dates;
              </li>

              <li>
                different job titles;
              </li>

              <li>
                inconsistent project names; and
              </li>

              <li>
                differing descriptions of responsibilities.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 7 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            07
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Review headings and navigation
            </h3>

            <p>
              Check the elements evaluators use to move
              through the proposal.
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                section headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                subsection headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                table of contents;
              </li>

              <li>
                page numbers;
              </li>

              <li>
                bookmarks;
              </li>

              <li>
                internal references;
              </li>

              <li>
                appendix references; and
              </li>

              <li>
                figure and table references.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <p>
              If the proposal says "see Section 4.2,"
              make sure Section 4.2 is actually where the
              reader is being sent.
            </p>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 8 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            08
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Proofread tables separately
            </h3>

            <p>
              Tables contain dense information and are easy
              to skim past during ordinary proofreading.
            </p>

            <p>
              Review:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                column headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                row headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                numbers;
              </li>

              <li>
                abbreviations;
              </li>

              <li>
                alignment;
              </li>

              <li>
                missing cells;
              </li>

              <li>
                broken rows;
              </li>

              <li>
                repeated information; and
              </li>

              <li>
                consistency with surrounding text.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 9 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            09
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Proofread graphics and captions
            </h3>

            <p>
              Text inside graphics often escapes normal
              spellcheck and editorial review.
            </p>

            <p>
              Check:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                labels;
              </li>

              <li>
                legends;
              </li>

              <li>
                dates;
              </li>

              <li>
                names;
              </li>

              <li>
                arrows and sequence;
              </li>

              <li>
                phase names;
              </li>

              <li>
                chart values;
              </li>

              <li>
                captions; and
              </li>

              <li>
                graphic references in the text.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 10 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            10
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Inspect page layout
            </h3>

            <p>
              Proofreading includes visual details.
            </p>

            <p>
              Review every page for:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                inconsistent margins;
              </li>

              <li>
                misaligned tables;
              </li>

              <li>
                awkward white space;
              </li>

              <li>
                orphaned headings;
              </li>

              <li>
                isolated lines;
              </li>

              <li>
                inconsistent fonts;
              </li>

              <li>
                unexpected font sizes;
              </li>

              <li>
                misplaced graphics;
              </li>

              <li>
                missing headers or footers; and
              </li>

              <li>
                inconsistent visual treatments.
              </li>

            </ul>

            <div class="sasha-practice-callout">

              <strong>
                This is detail-god territory
              </strong>

              <span>
                The table that is one millimetre out of
                alignment counts.
              </span>

            </div>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 11 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            11
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Check the first and last pages carefully
            </h3>

            <p>
              High-profile pages deserve an additional
              pass.
            </p>

            <p>
              Verify:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                cover information;
              </li>

              <li>
                client and project names;
              </li>

              <li>
                RFP number;
              </li>

              <li>
                submission date;
              </li>

              <li>
                contact information;
              </li>

              <li>
                signatures;
              </li>

              <li>
                closing information; and
              </li>

              <li>
                required declarations or forms.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


        <!-- STEP 12 -->

        <div class="sasha-practice-step-card">

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-number">
            12
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-step-content">

            <h3>
              Conduct a final clean-copy pass
            </h3>

            <p>
              After corrections have been made, review the
              final assembled proposal again.
            </p>

            <p>
              This pass is essential because correcting one
              problem can create another.
            </p>

            <p>
              Confirm that:
            </p>

            <ul>

              <li>
                corrections were actually applied;
              </li>

              <li>
                no text was accidentally deleted;
              </li>

              <li>
                pagination did not shift incorrectly;
              </li>

              <li>
                tables and graphics still display properly;
              </li>

              <li>
                cross-references remain correct; and
              </li>

              <li>
                the file being reviewed is the file that
                will actually be submitted.
              </li>

            </ul>

          </div>

        </div>


      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         HOW TO PROOFREAD
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Slow yourself down
        </h2>

        <p>
          Good proofreading requires a different reading
          style from ordinary reading.
        </p>

        <p>
          Useful techniques include:
        </p>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Read slowly rather than for meaning</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Review one type of error at a time</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Read headings separately from body copy</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Review tables independently</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Inspect numbers rather than simply reading them</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Check names every time they appear</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Zoom out to inspect page appearance</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Zoom in when reviewing visual details</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         SASHA ROLE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-value-panel">

        <div>

          <h2>
            Sasha's proofreading contribution
          </h2>

        </div>

        <div class="sasha-practice-value-copy">

          <p>
            Sasha can help perform a systematic text and
            consistency review of the final proposal.
          </p>

          <p>
            She can identify spelling, grammar, punctuation,
            terminology, capitalization, repetition,
            inconsistent names, questionable dates,
            conflicting numbers, unexplained acronyms,
            inconsistent titles, and other details that
            deserve attention.
          </p>

          <p>
            She can also compare repeated information
            across sections and flag differences for human
            verification.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sasha should not silently invent a correction
            when the underlying fact is uncertain. She
            should identify the discrepancy and ask the
            team to verify the correct information.
          </p>

          <p>
            Human proofreaders remain especially valuable
            for page-level visual review, final production
            judgment, and the tiny details that automated
            checks can miss.
          </p>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FINAL CHECKLIST
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Final proofreading checklist
        </h2>

        <div class="sasha-practice-check-grid">

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Spelling and grammar checked</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Regional spelling consistent</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Client and project names correct</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Personnel names and titles correct</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Dates and numbers verified</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Acronyms and terminology consistent</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Repeated facts agree throughout</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Table of contents correct</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Page numbers correct</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Cross-references checked</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Tables proofread</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Graphics and captions proofread</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Page layout inspected</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Headers and footers consistent</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Cover information verified</span>
          </div>

          <div class="sasha-practice-check-item">
            <span class="sasha-practice-check">✓</span>
            <span>Final corrected copy reviewed again</span>
          </div>

        </div>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         FINAL PRINCIPLE
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <h2>
          Finish like the details matter
        </h2>

        <p>
          By proofreading, the team is no longer trying to
          reinvent the proposal.
        </p>

        <p>
          The strategy has been developed. The content has
          been written. The reviews have been completed.
        </p>

        <p>
          The job now is to protect all that work by
          making sure the document leaving the organization
          is as disciplined and professional as the work
          it represents.
        </p>

      </div>

    </section>


    <!-- ============================================
         RETURN
    ============================================= -->

    <section class="sasha-practice-page-section">

      <div class="sasha-practice-content-card">

        <a
          href="/plan{{#if pursuitId}}?pursuit={{pursuitId}}{{/if}}"
        >
          Return to Plan
        </a>

      </div>

    </section>


  </div>

</div>